                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose capacity and runway`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `A scaling fintech just offered Foundry & Field its largest contract ever: <strong>EUR 1.2M over six months</strong>, with a hard, regulation-driven go-live date. Delivering it needs roughly <strong>double the delivery capacity</strong> — and you have 14 permanent staff, EUR 1.3M cash and a EUR 145,000 monthly burn, about <em>nine months of runway</em>. Quantify the gap and, crucially, split the demand: which of it is <strong>baseline</strong> (durable, justifies permanent hires) and which is <strong>surge</strong> (temporary, suits freelancers)? And decide how hard the runway constrains everything that follows.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the talent mix`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The core decision. Staff the five extra senior roles as <strong>employees or freelancers</strong>. An employee costs ~€8,500/month but carries a <em>3–6 month notice/severance tail</em>; a freelancer bills ~€11,500/month with <strong>zero tail and instant scale-down</strong>. The headline rate is a trap: for a six-month need, the “cheaper” employee can be the more expensive commitment. Run each role through four tests — cost per month, the commitment tail, ramp-up time, and is the capability <strong>core</strong> (own it) or <strong>contextual</strong> (rent it)? The two traps are an all-employee team that burns the runway and an all-freelance team that lets the platform knowledge walk out at month six.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Structure the team and the cash`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Operationalise the mix. First, the cash: the client pays at milestones but your team is paid monthly — a profitable contract still <em>kills you in month two</em> if the runway hits zero waiting for the first invoice. Structure the billing and, if needed, ring-fence a buffer so the runway curve never dips below zero. Second, the knowledge: critical platform capability can concentrate in 2–3 freelancers free to leave at handover. Decide how you capture it — IP clauses, pairing with the permanent core — so flexibility does not mean the expertise walks out the door.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Decide under a pipeline shock and pitch`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `<strong>The shock lands.</strong> One of the two EUR 500k Q3 follow-on deals is delayed indefinitely — the durable demand that might have justified permanent surge hires just shrank. The entire point of a flexible workforce is that it <em>flexes</em>: flex freelancers down, convert a key freelancer to lock in knowledge, or hold. Then an investor asks the only question that matters — <strong>does this plan extend your runway and prove your asset-light model, or does it bet the company?</strong> Stake the model on one metric and defend it.`,

                    r1_gap_label:           `How much extra delivery capacity does the 6-month contract actually need?`,
                    gap_under:              `A little more — stretch the current 14-person team with overtime; the contract isn't that big`,
                    gap_double:             `Roughly double delivery for six months — the build needs ~2× capacity, then it ends`,
                    gap_permanent:          `Double it permanently — win this and the pipeline will keep us at the new size for good`,
                    r1_split_label:         `How do you split the contract's demand?`,
                    split_allsurge:         `It's all surge — temporary work that disappears when the build ships in month six`,
                    split_allbaseline:      `It's all baseline — durable demand that justifies permanent hiring straight away`,
                    split_mixed:            `Mostly surge with a durable core — the platform's ownership and go-live support outlast the build`,
                    r1_runway_label:        `How seriously do you treat the runway as the binding constraint? (%)`,
                    r1_runway_readout:      `Runway discipline:`,
                    r1_pct:                 `%`,

                    r2_intro_label:         `Staff the surge: assign each of the 5 extra senior roles to employee or freelance. Employee ≈ €8,500/mo + a notice/severance tail; freelance ≈ €11,500/mo, zero tail, instant scale-down.`,
                    role_lead:              `Platform architect — owns the core design & go-live support (most strategic)`,
                    role_backend:           `Senior backend engineer — durable platform capability`,
                    role_data:              `Data/AI specialist — contextual, specialist, build-only`,
                    role_frontend:          `Front-end engineer — surge capacity for the build push`,
                    role_qa:                `QA / integration engineer — temporary, ends with the build`,
                    staff_employee:         `Employee`,
                    staff_freelance:        `Freelance`,
                    r2_mix_readout:         `Surge team mix:`,
                    r2_test_label:          `Which test drove your mix?`,
                    test_rate:              `Headline rate — employees bill less per month, so hire employees wherever I can`,
                    test_lean:              `Stay lean — freelance everything, carry zero permanent cost between projects`,
                    test_corecontext:       `Core-vs-contextual — own the strategic, IP-bearing roles; rent the temporary surge`,

                    r3_billing_label:       `Structure the client billing on the EUR 1.2M contract.`,
                    bill_endload:           `One invoice on go-live — bill the full EUR 1.2M when the platform ships in month six`,
                    bill_even:              `Even monthly — split the contract into six equal monthly invoices`,
                    bill_milestones:        `Front-loaded milestones — a deposit plus early milestones so cash arrives before payroll peaks`,
                    r3_knowledge_label:     `How do you stop critical platform knowledge walking out with a freelancer at month six?`,
                    know_none:              `Nothing formal — the freelancers are good, they'll document as they go`,
                    know_docs:              `A handover doc at the end — ask for written notes before contractors roll off`,
                    know_paired:            `Pair freelancers with the permanent core and write IP/handover clauses into every contract`,
                    r3_buffer_label:        `Cash buffer you ring-fence so runway never dips below zero waiting for a milestone (€000)`,
                    r3_buffer_hint:         `A profitable contract still kills you if payroll is monthly and the client pays late. A buffer (or a front-loaded milestone) keeps the runway curve above zero in the gap.`,

                    r4_shock:               `SHOCK — 12 March: one of the two EUR 500k Q3 follow-on deals is delayed indefinitely. The durable demand you may have hired against just got smaller. The whole point of a flexible workforce is that it flexes.`,
                    r4_flex_label:          `How do you respond to the pipeline shock?`,
                    flex_hold:              `Hold the full team — keep momentum, assume the deal comes back`,
                    flex_layoff:            `Lay off — cut permanent surge hires now and pay out their notice/severance tail`,
                    flex_flexdown:          `Flex the freelancers down and keep the owned core — use the reversibility you built in`,
                    flex_convert:           `Convert a key freelancer to permanent now to lock in the platform knowledge`,
                    r4_metric_label:        `Stake the model on ONE metric for the investor in the room.`,
                    metric_revenue:         `Revenue — “we just doubled quarterly revenue, the rest follows”`,
                    metric_headcount:       `Headcount — “we scaled the team, we look like a real company now”`,
                    metric_runway:          `Runway extended — “we delivered the build and still have a flexible cost base and months of runway”`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Pitch conviction: how firmly do you argue the asset-light model extended the runway rather than betting the company? (%)`,

                    warn_discipline_range:  `Runway discipline must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
