                    warn_buffer_range:      `The cash buffer must be between €0 and €600,000.`,
                    warn_pitch_range:       `Pitch conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_double:        `<strong>Right read on the gap.</strong> The contract needs roughly double the delivery capacity for six months — temporary by nature. Now the real question: how much of that doubling is durable demand you should own, and how much is surge you should rent?`,
                    coach_r1_under:         `<strong>Stretching 14 people with overtime won't hit a fixed, regulated go-live.</strong> Under-sizing the gap is how you miss the deadline that the whole contract hangs on. Be honest about the ~2× capacity the build needs.`,
                    coach_r1_permanent:     `<strong>“Double it permanently” is a bet on demand you haven't won yet.</strong> If you hire for the new size and the Q3 pipeline slips, you'll be carrying a payroll the runway can't fund. Size to the contract, not to the company you hope to become.`,
                    coach_r1_allsurge:      `<strong>Calling it all surge is half right.</strong> Most of it is temporary — but if you rent everything, who owns the platform architecture and go-live support after month six? Some of this is a durable core.`,
                    coach_r1_allbaseline:   `<strong>Treating it all as baseline justifies hiring everyone permanently.</strong> That's how the runway gets burned on a six-month need. Separate the durable core from the temporary surge before you staff anything.`,
                    coach_r1_nodiscipline:  `<strong>If the runway isn't the binding constraint, nothing stops you over-committing.</strong> On EUR 1.3M cash, every staffing choice is a cash choice. Treat the runway as the hard limit it is.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You sized the gap at ~2× for six months AND split it into a durable core plus a temporary surge. That's exactly the frame that lets you own what's strategic and rent what's temporary in the next round.`,
                    coach_r2_blend:         `<strong>A real blend.</strong> You've staffed a mix rather than swinging to an extreme. Make sure the two you own are the strategic, IP-bearing roles and the ones you rent are the temporary surge.`,
                    coach_r2_partial:       `<strong>Decision logged.</strong> Check the mix against the four tests: is each owned role genuinely core, and each rented role genuinely temporary? The line between own and rent is where this is won or lost.`,
                    coach_r2_allemp:        `<strong>You hired all five as permanent employees to “build the team.”</strong> Your burn jumps and the runway falls toward the cliff — and every one of them carries a notice/severance tail. If the Q3 deal slips you'll be laying off people you onboarded eight weeks ago. Which capability actually needed to be permanent?`,
                    coach_r2_allfree:       `<strong>All-freelance, maximum flexibility — clever, until month six.</strong> The people who hold the entire platform architecture invoice their last day and walk out, and the client's go-live support has no owner. What did you fail to make <em>core</em>? Flexibility isn't free when the knowledge leaves with it.`,
                    coach_r2_corecontext:   `<strong>That's the asset-light model done right.</strong> You own the strategic, IP-bearing roles and rent the temporary surge — the blend protects the runway, holds the knowledge, and proves freelancers are a growth driver, not a last resort.`,
                    coach_r2_rate:          `<strong>You chose employees because they “bill less per month.”</strong> Then the project ends and you owe months of notice and severance on roles you no longer need — real money for nothing. The freelancer's higher rate bought you a zero tail. For a six-month need, which was actually cheaper?`,
                    coach_r2_lean:          `<strong>Freelancing everything to stay lean keeps zero permanent cost — and zero owned capability.</strong> Lean isn't the goal; owning what's strategic while renting what's temporary is. Rent the surge, but make the core yours.`,
                    coach_r3_milestones:    `<strong>Front-loaded milestones are how you survive the timing gap.</strong> A deposit and early milestones land cash before payroll peaks, keeping the runway curve above zero. Profit didn't kill ventures; timing does — and you've handled it.`,
                    coach_r3_endload:       `<strong>One invoice on go-live is a cash-flow time bomb.</strong> Your team is paid monthly; if the only money arrives in month six, the runway hits zero in month two. The contract is profitable on paper and still sinks you. Phase the cash or ring-fence a real buffer.`,
                    coach_r3_paired:        `<strong>Pairing plus IP clauses keeps the knowledge in-house.</strong> Now the freelancers can roll off at month six without the platform expertise walking out with them — that's how asset-light stays safe.`,
                    coach_r3_solid:         `<strong>That's the operational backbone.</strong> Front-loaded cash keeps the runway positive through the gap, and pairing plus IP clauses keep the platform knowledge owned. The flexible model now has a spine.`,
                    coach_r3_noknow:        `<strong>“They'll document as they go” is how the expertise leaves with the invoice.</strong> Without pairing or IP clauses, the freelancers who hold the architecture walk out at handover and your go-live support has no owner. Capture the knowledge before it's gone.`,
                    coach_r4_flexdown:      `<strong>That's the model working.</strong> The pipeline shrank, so you flexed the freelancers down and kept the owned core — runway extended, knowledge retained, no severance tail. This is precisely why you built a flexible workforce.`,
                    coach_r4_nothingtoflex: `<strong>You want to flex down — but there's little to flex.</strong> If you'd staffed permanently, the only lever left is layoffs with a severance tail. The reversibility only exists if you rented the surge in the first place.`,
                    coach_r4_hold:          `<strong>You held the full team “to keep momentum.”</strong> The Q3 deal vanished and that decision just cost you runway you don't have. You built a flexible workforce and then refused to use the flexibility — that's the most expensive mistake of all.`,
                    coach_r4_layoff:        `<strong>Layoffs work, but they cost you the tail.</strong> You're paying out notice and severance on the surge hires — money you'd have saved by renting that capacity. Reversible commitments are worth a premium precisely so you never reach this point.`,
                    coach_r4_layoffheavy:   `<strong>This is the over-hiring bill coming due.</strong> You staffed the surge permanently, the pipeline slipped, and now you're paying out a heavy severance tail on people you onboarded weeks ago. The freelance premium would have been far cheaper than this.`,
                    coach_r4_convert:       `<strong>Converting a key freelancer makes sense here.</strong> You'd left the core under-owned, and locking in the platform knowledge before handover repairs the gap — owning what's strategic, even a round late.`,
                    coach_r4_overconvert:   `<strong>You already owned the core — converting now just adds permanent cost.</strong> The shock called for flexing down, not adding more fixed payroll. You're spending runway to solve a problem you'd already solved.`,
                    coach_r4_headcount:     `<strong>“We look like a real company now” is optimising for the wrong thing.</strong> Headcount isn't the metric; surviving is. An investor hears a bigger payroll and a shorter runway, not a stronger company.`,
                    coach_r4_investor:      `<strong>That's the pitch that lands.</strong> You delivered the build, flexed when demand shifted, and can stand in front of an investor and say the runway is longer and the model is proven. Freelancers were the growth driver, not the cost of last resort.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
