                    insight_proven:         `Asset-light proven: you sized the gap, owned the strategic core and rented the temporary surge, phased the cash against payroll, and flexed when the pipeline shifted. The contract delivered, the runway extended, and the model is something an investor can believe.`,
                    insight_overhired:      `Runway burned: you staffed temporary surge demand with permanent employees, the burn jumped, and the runway crossed the cliff. The notice and severance tails on hires you no longer need are the bill for optimising to look like a company instead of surviving as one.`,
                    insight_hollow:         `Core hollowed out: an all-freelance build maximised flexibility but left no owner for the platform architecture and go-live support. When the contractors rolled off, the knowledge walked out with them — flexibility wasn't free.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully reconcile the talent mix, the cash phasing and the shock response. The asset-light model only pays off when you own the core, rent the surge, fund the timing gap, and act on reversibility — and some of those threads stayed loose.`,
                    insight_cliff:          `Your runway crossed the under-five-month cliff. On a venture this is existential: a single slipped deal or late milestone now forces layoffs or a down-round. Staffing was a cash decision the whole time.`,
                    insight_runway_strong:  `Your runway held strong through the build — the surge was financed without crossing the cliff, which is exactly what the asset-light model is supposed to protect.`,
                    insight_margin_low:     `Project margin fell well below the 38% target — a freelance-heavy team protects flexibility but compresses margin, and that only pays off if you genuinely needed the reversibility.`,
                    insight_tail:           `You committed a sizeable notice/severance tail by hiring surge roles permanently. For a six-month need that tail is the hidden cost the headline rate never showed — the “cheaper” employee was the more expensive commitment.`,
                    insight_thesis:         `The thesis held: high-skilled freelancers attached to a discrete project let you flex up for a build and back down after it, faster and safer than an all-employee studio could. That is the project economy as a growth driver.`,

                    hint_cliff:             `Your runway is below the five-month cliff. Re-examine the mix and the cash: rent the temporary surge instead of hiring it permanently, and front-load the client milestones.`,
                    hint_hollow:            `You've freelanced the strategic core — the platform knowledge is set to walk out at month six. Own the architect and senior backend roles, or pair and write IP clauses to retain the expertise.`,
                    hint_overhired:         `You've over-hired permanent staff for temporary surge demand. The burn and the severance tail are both climbing — convert the model back toward renting the surge.`,
                    hint_cash:              `A profitable contract is heading for a cash crisis: payroll is monthly and your billing lands late. Front-load the milestones or ring-fence a buffer before the runway hits zero in the gap.`,
                    hint_conviction:        `The asset-light model isn't being proven. Own what's strategic, rent what's temporary, and be ready to flex when demand shifts — that's the conviction an investor is looking for.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — runway, delivery and conviction all healthy. Hold the blend, phase the cash, and be ready to flex the freelancers down if the pipeline moves.`,

